<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Han Fei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 280–233 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Han Feizi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Han Fei (c. 280–233 BCE) was a prince of the State of Han and the leading theorist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a>. The book named after him, the Han Feizi, argues that a ruler should govern through clear laws, strict rewards and punishments and techniques for controlling officials, rather than through Confucian virtue. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> as an envoy, he died in prison there, but his ideas shaped the policies of the Qin state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-fei.jpg" alt="Han Fei" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sabinowitz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Of_Han_Fei.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Han Fei was born into the ruling family of the State of Han in the last phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, which made him unlike most philosophers of his age, who came from outside the aristocracy. Scholars place his birth at about 280 BCE, though the exact year is unknown. Some have read his writings as addressed to his cousin, the King of Han. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>, compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, say that he studied alongside the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> under the Confucian philosopher Xun Kuang, and that a stutter kept him from presenting his ideas in court, though he wrote with great skill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Han Feizi is a later anthology traditionally attributed to him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> lists among its essays Solitary Indignation, Five Vermin, Inner and Outer Congeries of Sayings, Collected Persuasions and Difficulties in the Way of Persuasion, which he puts at about one hundred thousand words. Modern scholars differ over how much of the book is his; the sinologist Goldin has held that the outline of his life is probably not far from the truth, while Masayuki Sato doubted that all of Sima Qian's details are accurate.</p><p>Han Fei drew together the methods of earlier thinkers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a>'s emphasis on laws, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a>'s on administrative technique and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a>'s on authority. A ruler who mastered position or power (shi), technique (shu) and law (fa) could, in his view, hold firm control of the state. He stressed holding actual outcomes accountable to speech, joined to a system of two handles, punishment and reward. Like his teacher Xun Kuang he took human nature to be without virtue, but he proposed to steer people through their own self-interest rather than through moral education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> read Han Fei's works and said that if he could meet their author he might die without regrets. Han Fei came to the Qin court and presented an essay asking the king not to attack Han. Yao Gu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> used it to have him imprisoned on grounds of probable loyalty to his homeland, and Li Si then forced him to drink poison. The Qin king afterwards regretted his death. A different account, in the Stratagems of the Warring States, has the king order his execution after he slandered the Qin diplomat Yao Jia.</p><p>After the early fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, the succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> officially vilified the Legalist school. Even so, its ideas continued to influence later dynasties, and Han Fei's thought is sometimes compared with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Han Fei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Qin government was a bureaucracy of officials serving the emperor and put into practice the teachings of <b>Han Fei</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…associated with the school later called Legalism, preserved in the Book of Lord Shang and the writings of <b>Han Fei</b>, who died in Qin in 233 BCE; Confucian historians of the Han made Qin a byword for cruelty, a view that shaped Chinese political thought for two millennia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Classical Chinese is that of the great texts of the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, from the 6th to the 3rd centuries BCE: the Analects of Confucius, the Mencius, the Zhuangzi, the Xunzi, the <b>Han Feizi</b> and the Zuo Commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Their ideas shaped Shen Buhai, chancellor of the Han state; Shen Dao, who argued that the realm be modelled on the natural world; and <b>Han Fei</b>, whom many consider the greatest Legalist thinker.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mohists were wary of more atheistic thinkers of the time, such as <b>Han Fei</b>, and, given the vague nature of the records, the Mohist scribes themselves may not have been clear about spirits.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mohism">Mohism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Han Feizi</b> contains the earliest known commentaries on the text.</blockquote><a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinzheng">Xinzheng</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in the Xianyang County. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.4,113.73&amp;year=-250" aria-label="Xinzheng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Han Fei"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4558 4612h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4558 4612h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.4,113.73&amp;year=-250">What was here in 250 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So it is that the skilful doctor attacks disease while it is still in the muscles and easy to deal with.&#8221;</p><footer>Han Fei Tzŭ, the physician and Duke Huan (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; the moral Han Fei draws from the tale &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Therefore it is said that clever trickery is not equal to stupid sincerity.&#8221;</p><footer>Han Fei Tzŭ (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;So that when admonishing a ruler, it becomes necessary to consider the question of love or hatred before offering advice.&#8221;</p><footer>Han Fei Tzŭ, on persuading rulers (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A dragon is a deadly reptile which, however, can be trained to be fit for riding; but if a fishbone a foot long should stick in its throat and a man should try to remove it, there would be an end of the man.&#8221;</p><footer>Han Fei Tzŭ, on persuading rulers (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thus, when there is love for a ruler, wisdom steps in and familiarity is increased; but when there is hatred of a ruler, there comes cause for punishment and the result is alienation.&#8221;</p><footer>Han Fei Tzŭ, on persuading rulers (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A man spent three years in carving a leaf out of ivory, of such elegant and detailed workmanship that it would lie undetected among a heap of real leaves.&#8221;</p><footer>Han Fei Tzŭ, on leaving things to their natural course (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
